    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d657">

                <pb facs="0355" n="1445" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v06p2/pg1445"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">751.00/9–754: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 657<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to the Embassy in
                        France</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d657-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1954-09-09T19:43:00-04:00">September 9, 1954—7:43
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>896. Limit distribution. Bidault’s
                    attitude (Paris 1006<anchor n="2" xml:id="d657-f2"/>) and Chancellor’s (Bonn
                        698<anchor n="3" xml:id="d657-f3"/>) on desiring see Mendes forced out of <pb facs="0356"
                        n="1446" xml:id="pg1446"/> office are understandable in light EDC rejection but are disturbing from point of view
                    of accomplishing any solutions to problem German rearmament and German ties with
                    West.</p>
                <p>Regardless one’s opinion Mendes,
                    he is the one man in France we must deal with in coming critical weeks—his
                    strength in French Assembly appears not greatly diminished and greater than any
                    other potential Premier; furthermore, Assembly adjourned until November or when
                    he calls it back and he does enjoy some latitude of action. To work for his
                    downfall in hopes aftermath would provide answers seems destined destroy main
                    chance for early solution involving French cooperation. His professed views on
                    necessity German rearmament and rapid solution may be sincere in spite of his
                    distorted perspective and all the mistrust he has generated. Truth should become
                    apparent through his conduct rather quickly in coming weeks. While Bidault may prove to be right regarding
                        Mendes being neutralist,
                    former’s approach also appears put internal political considerations above
                    interests of France at this juncture (same accusation leveled at Mendes). For if Mendes does prove to be sincere and
                    determined to push for early solution, and if he is not supported by men such as
                        Bidault in efforts obtain
                    parliamentary approval attempted solutions worked out in coming weeks, results
                    would be extremely serious for entire Western Alliance and France in
                    particular.</p>
                <p>Believe foregoing points should be made to Bidault and at Embassy’s discretion to Mollet, Pinay, Reynaud and
                    other pro-EDC leaders who bound to be bitter at
                        Mendes. Conant should also take advantage
                    appropriate opportunity explain to Chancellor our views on necessity attempting
                    do business in coming weeks with Mendes.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Smith</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d657-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d657-f1-content">
                <p>Drafted by Fisher and cleared with
                        Kidd, Merchant, Murphy, Matthews, and RA. Repeated to
                    Bonn for action and to London for information.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d657-f2" n="2" xml:id="d657-f2-content">
                <p>This telegram summarized a private conversation between Bidault and Dillon during which Bidault criticized Mendès-France and stated that the only
                    real solution regarding German rearmament would be one which helped Adenauer and forced Mendès-France out of office.
                    (751.00/9–754)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d657-f3" n="3" xml:id="d657-f3-content">
                <p>For text of this telegram, dated Sept. 7, which summarized a conversation between
                    Chancellor Adenauer and Conant on Sept. 7, see <ref
                        target="frus1952-54v05p2#pg_1152">vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">v</hi>,
                        Part 2, p. 1152</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
